            <ArticleTitle>Psychological aspects of components of pain.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>135-46</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>The present study investigated the inhibitory effects of acupuncture on the separate sensory and emotional components of pain and their relation to each other. Electro-acupuncture as practiced in China was applied to the same nerve used for initiating pain, and the responses of 9 adult volunteers (5 males, 4 females) were assessed. The results indicate that (a) pain involves at least two related but different components (the sensory component and the emotional component) and (b) electro-acupuncture has a stronger inhibitory effect on the emotional component of pain. The findings provide a new outlook for the improvement of the clinical effects of acupuncture and for further study of the pain mechanism.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
